  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(6)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =(6)^{2}-5(6)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t+4=(t-1)(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/ad76f817f7c6e4a254e8881c34270eb875e72d821531566cb141cb393dcead72.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<4.$",
